      "dimension_name": "Sequencing logic",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "The chronological order (1870 → 1880 → 1900) is sound, and the plan correctly skips 1850/1860 and 1890. But it includes no Ancestry fallback items, and every fallback_for is null. That fails the required fallback_for chain, and the free-before-paid ordering is only met because there’s nothing paid to order against.\n\nOne small correction to the LLM’s reasoning: the plan doesn’t violate the FamilySearch-before-Ancestry rule. It meets it by default, since no Ancestry items exist. The real failure is the missing fallback chain."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_011",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Completeness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "All three requested census years are covered with clear rationales, and 1850/1860 aren’t re-searched. The only gap is the required Ancestry fallback_for chain, which is entirely missing."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_011",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "The LLM’s rationale is wrong. pli_007 and pli_009 aren’t Ancestry items; they’re FamilySearch searches for Thomas. The missing fallback chain is a completeness issue, not a correctness one. The plan’s facts are accurate: the collection IDs, the 1870 census having no relationship column, 1880 being first with relationships and parents’ birthplaces, the 1890 fire, and the 1900 birth month/year. One minor error: pli_007 claims Thomas’s age and birthplace weren’t recorded in 1850 and 1860, but those censuses did record both for him."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_003",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Tool Arguments",
      "llm_score": 3,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": null
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_003",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Completeness",
      "llm_score": 1,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "The LLM’s rationale is wrong: the skill did decline. It made no research_append call and added no plan items. It explicitly said “no new plan item is needed,” cited the existing exhaustive_declaration, and routed to sensible next steps (the proof critique on ps_001 and the q_002 proof summary). The judge context allows “otherwise decline to plan, citing that the question is resolved,” which is exactly what happened. Reading the project state to justify the decline isn’t the same as doing research-plan’s task."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_003",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 1,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "The LLM even concedes the response “is factually accurate about the project state.” Its claims match the project data: pl_002 is complete, pli_006 returned a nil probate result, the exhaustive_declaration is set, ps_001 hasn’t been critiqued, and q_002 has no proof summary. The skill also declined correctly, with no plan item created and a clear “we’re done planning.” Answering “no, don’t add an item” is the decline the test expects, not a failure to decline."
    },
